# [EODIR](../wg/eodir.html)

## Summary

The Education, Outreach, and Diversity Directorate (EODIR) met at IETF 127. The session was chaired by Karen O'Donoghue. The meeting covered updates on regional community outreach initiatives, a debrief and future planning for the GitHub web interface authoring tutorial, reports on the Working Group Chairs Forum and Sisters activities, progress on IAB outreach coordination, reports from the New Participants and Guides programs, a framework for student participation tracking, and a discussion on providing tooling refreshers for experienced IETF participants.

---

## Key Discussion Points

### Regional Outreach and Community Events
* **IIESOC & APNIC 62**: Mohit (India Internet Engineering Society - IIESOC) reported on the in-person "RFCs We Love" meetup co-hosted alongside APNIC 62 in India (September 2026). The 90-minute evening session had 45+ attendees and included an introduction to the IETF followed by talks from Suresh Krishnan (AI in the IETF) and Tom Harrison (APNIC, on Autonomous System Provider Authorization / ASPA). IIESOC also continues running post-quantum cryptography webinars with the Industry Network Technology Council (INTC).
* **Asia Interface Foundation**: Jean Francois (Asia Interface Foundation) reported on fellowship activities. While no fellows attended IETF 127 in person due to travel and time zone constraints, 7 fellows (2 lecturers, 5 students) attended ICANN 87 in Bali. 
* **Preparations for IETF 128 (Kuala Lumpur)**:
  * Two fellowship tracks will be offered: standard participation fellowships and 3 dedicated fellows supporting the Network Operations Center (NOC).
  * A Hackathon/Code Sprint prep session will be organized in collaboration with local universities.
  * An Open Day is planned for January 12 to engage local academic, government, and industry stakeholders prior to the meeting.

### Hands-on GitHub Authoring Tutorial Report-Back
* Karen O'Donoghue, Mirja Kühlewind, and Lucas Pardue debriefed the Sunday hands-on tutorial on authoring Internet-Drafts using the GitHub web interface. Materials available on the Datatracker:
  * [Hands-on tutorial - Using the GitHub web interface for authoring Internet-Drafts (slides)](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-eodir-sessc-hands-on-tutorial-using-the-github-web-interface-for-authoring-internet-drafts-slides-01)
  * [GitHub Web Interface for Internet-Draft Authoring - Cheat Sheet](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-eodir-sessc-github-web-interface-for-internet-draft-authoring-cheat-sheet-00)
* **Feedback & Insights**:
  * Attendance was approximately 14–15 participants.
  * The audience was diverse, ranging from complete beginners to experienced co-authors wanting to understand repository setup and Martin Thompson's draft template.
  * Lucas Pardue highlighted that instructors must clearly clarify that GitHub and Markdown are optional tooling choices rather than mandatory IETF processes.
* **Format Adjustments**:
  * Mirja Kühlewind proposed restructuring future sessions into three separate, modular shorter slots:
    1. Markdown authoring/editing basics (collaboration proposed with Alice Russo).
    2. Opening issues and Pull Requests (aimed at draft reviewers and commenters).
    3. Advanced setup and workflow using the Martin Thompson template.

### Working Group Chairs Forum Update
* Karen O'Donoghue and Rich Salz summarized the Working Group Chairs Forum:
  * Featured a Q&A session with IETF Legal Counsel.
  * Presentation on the moderation process by Bron Gondwana.
  * Updates on IAB Outreach by Yaroslav Rosomakho, RFC Editor website by Alexis, and the WG Chair History Project by Robert Sparks.

### Sisters & Diversity Activities
* Karen O'Donoghue reported on the Sisters events (Monday breakfast and Thursday lunch, assisted by Laura). 
* Discussions focused heavily on the Nominating Committee (NomCom) process, leadership selection, and strategies for encouraging more female candidates to stand for IESG/IAB nominations.

### IAB & Community Outreach Coordination
* Yaroslav Rosomakho (Outreach Coordinator) provided an update on cross-community outreach:
  * **Operator Communities**: Regular updates at RIPE, NANOG, and APRICOT. Jen Linkova is organizing a v6ops interim meeting co-located with an upcoming RIPE meeting.
  * **Policy & Civil Society**: Workshops planned for the upcoming IGF meeting.
  * **Domain-Specific Conferences**: Sharing IETF technical topics at specialized security events and technical forums (e.g., JSON Schema updates).
* **Outreach Wiki**: Dhruv Dhody highlighted the [Outreach Wiki](https://wiki.ietf.org/group/eodir/outreach), which now features an upcoming events table allowing community members to coordinate activities at external events (e.g., IGF, RIPE).

### New Participants and Guides Programs
* **New Participants Program**: Michelle Cotton (Secretariat) reported strong engagement with over 50 attendees at the Sunday session. The Quick Connections event remains highly popular, though room capacity needs evaluation for future meetings.
* **Self-Test MeetEcho Access**: Robert Sparks and Greg Wood clarified that WG chairs and leadership can access self-test MeetEcho rooms via the [MeetEcho Admin Interface](https://ws-interims.conf.meetecho.com/admin-ietf/).
* **Guides Program**: Wes Hardaker reported 20 guide-participant pairings (18 in-person, 2 remote). Wes noted plans to issue a call for guides to update their profile interest areas (adding topics like AI and Post-Quantum Cryptography) and recruit past program participants as new guides.
* **Work-Life Balance Session Idea**: Raised by Robert Sparks and discussed by Karen O'Donoghue and Mirja Kühlewind, there is community interest in hosting a dedicated session or GEN area panel on balancing IETF work with day-job commitments.

### Student Program Impact Survey Framework
* Akansha Gandotra presented a standardized survey template designed for student program organizers (e.g., IIESOC, Asia Interface Foundation).
* The framework aims to collect translated English survey responses across multiple independent student initiatives to build a centralized, long-term dataset on how student participation programs impact ongoing IETF engagement.

### Tooling Refreshers for Experienced Participants ("Elders")
* John Klensin raised a concern that long-time IETF participants—particularly those attending remotely—often miss out on incremental updates to tools and operational workflows. 
* Deb Cooley and Mirja Kühlewind agreed that offering focused tooling refreshers or broader panel formats for experienced participants (beyond the WG Chairs Forum) would be beneficial.

